WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.033
-Welcome, welcome, welcome
to the show.

00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:03.200
It very nice to meet you.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.267
-Likewise.
-You look fantastic.

00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:06.868
-Thank you.
-In addition to being

00:00:06.868 --> 00:00:10.234
a great actor,
you're also a stand-up comic,

00:00:10.234 --> 00:00:12.367
which I think

00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:14.033
helps with the show, probably.
-Sure.

00:00:14.033 --> 00:00:17.367
Doesn't hurt.
-Are you doing shows
this summer?

00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:20.100
-I am. I'm going on the road.
-Do you like going on the road?

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.300
-I do. You know, it's similar --
a little bit different.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.868
I don't travel quite
in as luxurious style

00:00:25.868 --> 00:00:28.067
as Deborah Vance.
-Yeah. You don't.

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:29.634
Deborah Vance,
the fictional character,

00:00:29.634 --> 00:00:32.400
her motto is, "Less is less"...
-Uh-huh.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.567
[ Laughter ]
-and my motto is, of course,

00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:37.667
"The exit row is the first class
of coach."

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:38.834
[ Laughter ]

00:00:38.834 --> 00:00:41.067
So.
-It actually is, yeah.

00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:42.868
It really is.
-I mean, think about it.

00:00:42.868 --> 00:00:44.701
-Of course, I always ask
for the exit row, yeah.
-That's what I'm telling myself.

00:00:44.701 --> 00:00:47.367
-It's a lot of pressure, though,
I feel, for me, because like

00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:49.734
I have to save everyone's life,
in case of emergency.

00:00:49.734 --> 00:00:51.400
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, but wouldn't you
rather it be you

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.801
than like some guy?
-You know, yeah, always.

00:00:53.801 --> 00:00:55.100
I'm like, "Hey, guys,
you're in good hands."

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.534
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.
-"I actually listened

00:00:56.534 --> 00:00:58.000
to everyone.
I know how to open this door."

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.701
-If you saved my life,
I'd be like,

00:01:00.701 --> 00:01:03.133
"Yeah, the plane went down.
but Jimmy Fallon."

00:01:03.133 --> 00:01:04.267
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:05.667
-"Literally."
-How long

00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:07.901
will you be on the road for?

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.501
-Oof!
You know how on-the-road time

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:12.334
is like such an --
such a -- such a weird --

00:01:12.334 --> 00:01:14.834
I mean, is time a construct,
as we all know.

00:01:14.834 --> 00:01:15.968
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:15.968 --> 00:01:18.067
-But on the road,
it gets a little funky.

00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:21.300
I believe it's roughly July...
to September?

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.934
I'm hitting like places
around the States

00:01:23.934 --> 00:01:27.501
and then going
to London and Canada.

00:01:27.501 --> 00:01:30.300
But it does get a little like
I don't know where I am

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.801
or when I am sometimes
when I'm out there.

00:01:32.801 --> 00:01:34.133
-When I am.
-[Both] Yeah.

00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:36.200
-Yeah, like I was in D.C.
last week --

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.834
or maybe two weeks ago --

00:01:37.834 --> 00:01:39.200
or, actually, I guess
it could be a year.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.567
Again, not good with time.

00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:42.000
[ Laughter ]
-Wow, yeah, you're really --
Yeah.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.000
But I was in D.C.
and I was like

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.601
kind of giving the crowd
a little bit of grief.

00:01:45.601 --> 00:01:46.901
I was like,
"You guys aren't partying?

00:01:46.901 --> 00:01:48.734
What's going on?
Like it's Saturday night."

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:50.501
And they were all like,
"It's Thursday!"

00:01:50.501 --> 00:01:52.367
[ Laughter ]
-Wow. In unison.

00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:53.667
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:55.367
-Congrats on "Hacks,"
by the way.

00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:57.234
-Thank you.
-You're fantastic.
It's your first TV show.

00:01:57.234 --> 00:02:00.267
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:03.434
Emmy-nominated.
Not too shabby for your first.

00:02:03.434 --> 00:02:06.801
But you play a comedy writer,
which is a tough gig.

00:02:06.801 --> 00:02:10.000
As a comic, did you feel like
you would look the part,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.667
when you were auditioning,
You're like, "I can do this.

00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:13.400
I know how to do this"?
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.200
No. I --
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.567
There was no -- Honestly,
this is like the first time

00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:19.968
my low self-esteem
has ever come in handy.

00:02:19.968 --> 00:02:21.601
[ Laughter ]
-Really?
-Yeah, because I was just like,

00:02:21.601 --> 00:02:23.701
"There's no way
I'm booking this.

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:25.033
Better just have fun with it."

00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:26.567
-Yeah.
-And it -- it kind of like

00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:27.701
ended up helping me, in the end,

00:02:27.701 --> 00:02:29.601
because I was a little bit
more free.

00:02:29.601 --> 00:02:32.300
-You said the screen test was
you and Jean Smart,

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.501
who's also brilliant as well.
-Yes.

00:02:34.501 --> 00:02:36.200
-What was that like?

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.934
-It was bizarre.
It was --

00:02:37.934 --> 00:02:42.367
This is before the vaccine,
during the COVID-19 pandemic

00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:44.000
that, of course,
is still going on.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:45.968
But just in the beginning

00:02:45.968 --> 00:02:49.400
and it was on a dark soundstage
at Paramount,

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.534
a completely empty soundstage.
For those who don't know,

00:02:51.534 --> 00:02:54.501
it's essentially a warehouse
where they build sets.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.501 --> 00:02:56.267
-So you went
into a dark warehouse?

00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:57.634
-I did.
-Yeah.

00:02:57.634 --> 00:03:01.200
-Only two like interrogation
lights on Jean and I

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.767
and like nine producers
in the dark.

00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:05.701
And I kind of just walked in
being like, "Okay,

00:03:05.701 --> 00:03:08.300
so, this was all a ruse
to murder me. Well played!"

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:09.868
[ Laughter ]
-You actually brought a photo

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:11.968
-Yeah.
-and it's pretty --

00:03:11.968 --> 00:03:13.534
You nailed it. It's frightening.
-Yeah.

00:03:13.534 --> 00:03:15.133
-Yeah.
-The Plexiglas

00:03:15.133 --> 00:03:16.734
between Jean and I
and she's obviously giving me

00:03:16.734 --> 00:03:19.834
a little smirk like, "This
little young girl's got it,"

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:22.601
or whatever --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:24.000
-People love the show.
It received

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.968
15 Emmy nominations last year.

00:03:25.968 --> 00:03:27.100
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.033
15!

00:03:29.033 --> 00:03:32.033
It won a Peabody Award.
-Insane.

00:03:32.033 --> 00:03:35.434
-And both seasons are 100%
on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:03:35.434 --> 00:03:36.767
[ Cheering and applause ]
How does that -- That's --

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:39.133
Yeah, "Hacks."

00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:41.901
It is very funny.
It's a comedy.

00:03:41.901 --> 00:03:44.300
But there's also some
very dramatic moments in there,

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.267
very emotional moments.

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:49.033
Do you like getting into the --
the drama and the acting?

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:51.234
-I do. I love drama.
-You do? Yeah.

00:03:51.234 --> 00:03:52.367
I know.
-Just as a concept.

00:03:52.367 --> 00:03:53.601
[ Laughter ]
-I love drama, too, yeah.

00:03:53.601 --> 00:03:56.100
-Well, but I think like
any accurate depiction

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.501
of a life of any person

00:03:57.501 --> 00:03:59.634
has an equal amount of comedy
and drama in it,

00:03:59.634 --> 00:04:01.734
if, you know,
if you're paying attention.

00:04:01.734 --> 00:04:04.267
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]
-And -- [ Laughs ]

00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:07.267
And so a lot of people do ask
me like, you know,

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:09.667
"How are you able
to sort of like get

00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:12.234
into the more emotional moments
if you have like

00:04:12.234 --> 00:04:14.267
so little acting experience?"
And I just want to be clear,

00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:16.300
I have cried at every job
I've ever had.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:17.567
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:19.234
-No matter what job.
-The whole crying at work thing?

00:04:19.234 --> 00:04:21.467
Not new for me, you know?
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:23.934
-You cry at all jobs.
-Yeah. I mean,

00:04:23.934 --> 00:04:26.534
is there an American across this
great nation who doesn't?

00:04:26.534 --> 00:04:28.334
[ Laughter ]
-Alright, yeah. No, yeah, yeah.
-Folks?

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:29.901
-Everyone here cries, yeah.
-We're all crying at work...

00:04:29.901 --> 00:04:31.934
[ Cheering and applause ]
-We had a good cry.
-...right? Yes!

00:04:31.934 --> 00:04:35.000
-I -- Like before,
when I was a barista,

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.367
I would just cry
in the storage room

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:40.133
between an industrial fridge
and a bunch of coffee beans

00:04:40.133 --> 00:04:42.167
and they'd be like, "Hannah!
Your ten-minute break is over.

00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:44.000
Get back to work
and stop crying."

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.434
Now, I'm crying on set
and they're like,

00:04:45.434 --> 00:04:47.467
"Yes! More! Again! Amazing!

00:04:47.467 --> 00:04:49.167
[ Laughter and applause ]
Queen!"

00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:51.067
-Yes, queen.
Yes, queen.

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:52.400
That's hilarious.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:53.767
Well, I just want to say that

00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:56.467
"Hacks" is officially picked up
for a third season,

00:04:56.467 --> 00:04:58.100
so, congratulations on that.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:58.767
[ Rimshot ]
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:00.968
-You're fantastic in it.

00:05:00.968 --> 00:05:02.234
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:05:02.234 --> 00:05:04.734
Here's Hannah Einbinder
in Season 2 of "Hacks."

00:05:04.734 --> 00:05:06.501
Take a look at this.

00:05:06.501 --> 00:05:08.300
-You know what, Deborah?

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.067
You're orientation isn't gay
or straight.

00:05:12.467 --> 00:05:14.033
It's egomaniac.

00:05:14.734 --> 00:05:17.167
-[ Scoff ] Ah, this waistband
is kind of loose.

00:05:17.167 --> 00:05:19.300
-[ Sigh ]

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.434
-Ah, should've done
another round of testing.

00:05:22.434 --> 00:05:24.367
-♪ Someone marches ♪

00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:26.734
-Oh, no.
-♪ Brave ♪

00:05:26.734 --> 00:05:28.267
♪ Here ♪

00:05:28.267 --> 00:05:29.968
-Molly's hitting.
-♪ Beneath ♪

00:05:29.968 --> 00:05:30.701
-What?
-♪ My ♪

00:05:30.701 --> 00:05:31.801
-I might have to dance.

00:05:31.801 --> 00:05:34.300
-Molly?
-♪ Skin ♪

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:38.334
♪ And constant ♪
-Okay, I can't watch this.

00:05:38.334 --> 00:05:41.901
-♪ Craving ♪

00:05:41.901 --> 00:05:44.234
♪ Has al... ♪
-[ Gasp ] Ooh, ooh!

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:46.234
-♪ ...ways ♪
-Is that a dolphin?

00:05:46.234 --> 00:05:47.767
Right there.
-♪ Been ♪

00:05:47.767 --> 00:05:49.701
-No, that's trash.
-Agh! God.

00:05:49.701 --> 00:05:51.000
[ Laughter ]

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.367
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Hannah Einbinder, everyone!

00:05:53.367 --> 00:05:57.100
Seasons 1 and 2 of "Hacks"
are streaming now on HBO Max.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:58.834
Mo Willems joins us
after the break.

00:05:58.834 --> 00:05:59.968
Stick around.

